<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khumarawayh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/864">864</a>–<a href="/years/896">896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Khumarawayh ibn Ahmad ibn Tulun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khumarawayh was the second Tulunid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a> in 884. After fighting the Abbasid regent al-Muwaffaq, he reached a treaty in 886 that recognised his rule over Egypt and Syria in return for tribute. He married his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatr-al-nada">Qatr al-Nada</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> with a famously lavish dowry, but his extravagance emptied the treasury, and he was murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khumarawayh.jpg" alt="Khumarawayh" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, 9th-century mint masters · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Khumarawayh_ibn_Ahmad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu 'l-Jaysh Khumarawayh was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in 864. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a>, the son of a Turkish slave-soldier, became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 868, took direct control of its revenue in 871 and used it to build his own army of slave soldiers, the ghilmān. Exploiting the rift between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mu'tamid and his brother al-Muwaffaq, he extended his rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the Byzantine frontier zone and parts of the Jazira. In 882, after the failed rebellion of his elder brother Abbas, Khumarawayh was named his father's deputy in Egypt and heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His succession in May 884 was, in the view of the historian Thierry Bianquis, the first time a governor of so large a territory was openly succeeded by an amir claiming legitimacy by inheritance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> attacked his lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and al-Muwaffaq's son Abu'l-Abbas, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a>, took charge of the invasion; at the Battle of Tawahin Khumarawayh first fled, but his general Sa'd al-Aysar routed the Abbasid prince. He then suppressed al-Aysar's revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and treated the prisoners with clemency, which led to the agreement of December 886. Between 886 and 890 he received the submission of the Jazira, and the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> accepted his overlordship.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 892, the pact was renewed in 893 for an annual tribute of 300,000 dinars and the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-rabia">Diyar Rabi'a</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-mudar">Diyar Mudar</a>. At home Khumarawayh built palaces, kept a blue-eyed lion and was rocked to sleep on a basin of quicksilver; he was also a patron of scholars and poets. Unable to command the army as his father had, he bought its loyalty with rich gifts, and by his death the treasury, said to have held ten million gold dinars at his accession, was empty and the dinar had lost two-thirds of its value.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A servant who had been having an affair with Khumarawayh's favourite wife organised a conspiracy and had him killed on 18 January 896. His son Jaysh was deposed and killed in November in favour of his younger brother Harun. Al-Mu'tadid took back the Thughūr and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, and in 904–905 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Muktafi reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The lavish wedding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatr-al-nada">Qatr al-Nada</a> remained the stuff of folk legend in Egypt into the Ottoman period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khumarawayh ibn Ahmad ibn Tulun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Khumarawayh</b> made a treaty with the caliph in 886 and married his daughter Qatr al-Nada to al-Mu'tadid with a famously lavish dowry.</blockquote><a href="/state/tulunid-emirate">Tulunid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qatai">Al-Qatta'i</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.2,43.89&amp;year=884" aria-label="Samarra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5667 925 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Khumarawayh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6117 1195h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6117 1195h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.2,43.89&amp;year=884">What was here in 884 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
